      ":db/id": 17592186087428,
      ":question/id": "62f7d6e1-61ec-5f3d-8cdd-4da0c90c7ccf",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "e-1222",
      ":question/problem": "The graph below shows $y=k(6^{x})+c.$ Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve.\n\n![](images/de/deb7e20658882229a3653f4058c787bf2a5c4a26c5f2982406a2dba34c2d5846.png)",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k(6^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=4$, so $c=4.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k(6^{x})+4.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,6),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the function and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({6}^{x})+4 \\\\ 6 & =k({6}^{0})+4 \\\\ 6 & =k(1)+4 \\\\ 6 & =k+4 \\\\ 2 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=2(6^{x})+4.$"
    },
    ":knowledge-point/id": "0b70a3b7-90a7-5a83-b71f-304f7f25c4ef",
